th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028","history_only":true,"curation_sha256":"402117cd2ecc28eaf6387aaefcadec5f0c0540f0912f8a96983845daf37ec8e2","concept_id":"Year-2/2b-Game-Theory/subgame-perfect-nash-equilibrium.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-2/2b-Game-Theory/subgame-perfect-nash-equilibrium.html","questions":[{"id":"year_2_2b_game_theory_subgame_perfect_nash_equilibrium_f7b86f1e_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Subgame perfection refines Nash equilibrium in extensive-form games. What kind of behaviour does it rule out that Nash equilibrium allows?","options":["Behaviour that gives some player a payoff lower than she would get in another Nash equilibrium.","Non-optimal behaviour at unreached information sets, such as threats that would not be carried out.","Any use of mixed strategies, since subgame perfection requires every player to use pure strategies.","Behaviour on the path of play that is not a best response to the other players' strategies."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6bf713c57c6f3822002a8da8","time_limit":35,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_2_2b_game_theory_subgame_perfect_nash_equilibrium_f7b86f1e_008","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"In a trust game, player 1 chooses Keep, giving $(1,1)$, or Trust; after Trust, player 2 chooses Share, $(2,2)$, or Grab, $(0,3)$. Which profile is the subgame-perfect Nash equilibrium?","options":["(Trust, Share)","(Trust, Grab)","(Keep, Share)","(Keep, Grab)"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"798226b6472522603ca07cb5","time_limit":25,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_2_2b_game_theory_subgame_perfect_nash_equilibrium_f7b86f1e_010","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Player 1 takes Out, with payoffs $(2,2)$, or In; then player 1 picks $A$ or $B$ while player 2 simultaneously picks $a$ or $b$: $(A,a)\\mapsto(3,1)$, $(B,b)\\mapsto(1,3)$, and $(0,0)$ after a mismatch. Consider the SPNE in which the subgame after In is played as $(A,a)$. What is the outcome of this SPNE?","options":["$(2,2)$, since Out is the safe choice.","$(1,3)$, since player 2 moves last.","$(0,0)$, since play miscoordinates.","$(3,1)$, since In gives player 1 $3>2$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2db2b39d49f710480c89e94f","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_2_2b_game_theory_subgame_perfect_nash_equilibrium_f7b86f1e_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Consider a finite extensive-form game of perfect information in which no player has the same payoff at two different terminal nodes. How many pure-strategy subgame-perfect Nash equilibria does it have?","options":["At least two.","Possibly none.","One per player.","Exactly one."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d2c9e8ceb8fac670e960c507","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_2_2b_game_theory_subgame_perfect_nash_equilibrium_f7b86f1e_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For finite games of perfect information, \"no player has the same payoff at two different terminal nodes\" guarantees a unique pure SPNE. Must a perfect-information game in which some player has equal payoffs at two terminal nodes have several pure SPNE?","options":["Yes: any equal payoffs create a tie, and every tie produces a second pure SPNE.","Yes: without the condition no pure SPNE need exist, let alone a unique one.","No: equal payoffs never affect the number of SPNE in a game of perfect information.","No: it is sufficient, not necessary; only a mover's indifference at a node creates extra SPNE."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1a28611c1976f9bcfc2b6a7b","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"}]}}